# Context Metrics and Removal — measure, remove, evaluate

Status: proposed — nothing built. This implements the intent-network's
measurement family (../intent-network/04-sidecar, 05-evaluation-layer,
08-context-mechanisms) inside the manage plugin + state.db.

Supersedes: doc 18's S4 (digests + prune, implemented here as C2) and S6
(skill tiers — reduced to machine tags), and doc 10 (skills gating — reduced
to capture plus optional validation). Wording/injection is deliberately not
the mechanism: intent is measured, not prompted.

## The problem

Context only grows. Every turn adds tool output, dead ends, superseded code,
and plan text; nothing removes any of it. Injected prose ("stay in scope",
skill routing hints) is a nudge the model may ignore — the framework verdict:
intent must be a measured quantity, not a prompt. Removal must be structural
and its effect must be measurable, or it is theater.

## What exists today (verified)

| Layer | State |
|---|---|
| Verdict events (accept/reject/correct/abort) in `state.db` | working (S0, 2026-09-13) |
| Proposals + journal | working |
| Token/context metrics | none — the SDK exposes per-message tokens; nothing records them |
| Removal mechanisms | none — no prune, no compaction shaping |
| Evaluation / retention tiers | none — docs only (intent-network 04/05/08) |

## Architecture

```
session events ──► manage.ts hooks ──► state.db (turn_metrics, context_events, verdicts)
                                              │
                    nvim console reads ◄──────┘
                                              │
plugin hooks (messages.transform, compacting) ◄── removal reads metrics + verdicts
```

- Capture is plugin-only (event hook + tool hooks). No new process.
- Storage is the existing `state.db` (project + global), additive tables only.
- Removal reads metrics + verdicts; it never asks the model to decide.

## C1 — Measure (automatic)

Additive tables (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` on both sides):

| Table | Row per | Columns |
|---|---|---|
| turn_metrics | assistant message (upsert by message_id) | session_id, message_id, ts, tokens_input, tokens_output, cache_read, cache_write, model, agent |
| context_events | notable act | session_id, ts, kind (tool_call / skill_load / proposal / prune / compaction), detail, subject |

Capture points in manage.ts (all existing hooks):
- `event` on `message.updated` — assistant token usage; fail-open when a
  provider omits usage fields.
- `tool.execute.after` — tool name + result status (no payload bodies).
- `tool.execute.before` on `skill` — skill name, plus subject tags from the
  policy manifest below.
- `emit_proposal` / `registry_propose` execute — one context_event row
  alongside the queue write.
- Verdicts stay in the verdicts table; evaluation reads them, no duplication.

Aggregation: SQL views (`v_session_growth`, `v_tool_usage`) so the plugin and
nvim read the same numbers; no cached copies.

**Skills policy manifest** (the only skill change): `skills.policy.json`
(global + optional per-project merge) mapping `skill -> { subject[] }` —
machine tags so skill loads are attributable. No routing prose, no tier
injection. Structured validation of skills remains optional here.

Acceptance (runtime, not doc status): after a >3-turn session,
`turn_metrics` has one row per assistant message with non-zero tokens, and
the growth curve is queryable per session.

## C2 — Remove (the first real mechanism)

Removal levers (plugin-only, verified in the SDK):

- `experimental.session.compacting` — shape compaction: inject slice digests
  and the instruction to drop exploration and superseded code while keeping
  verified state.
- `experimental.chat.messages.transform` — coarse removal on the outgoing
  array: replace old, verified-slice text parts with their digest; never
  touch the last N turns; never touch toolcall/result pairs until proven;
  fail-open (any error returns the array unmodified).
- Trigger: user action in the nvim console first (deterministic); an
  automatic token-threshold trigger only after the manual path proves safe.

Prune candidates come from data, not judgment: proposal groups that are
verified (S0 readback) and older than K turns; paths whose verdict history is
rejected or corrected; content superseded by a later accepted proposal.

Digests (doc 18 S4): written at verify time — files changed with hashes,
decisions, verified checks, superseded approaches. The digest is what
survives; detail is what goes.

Acceptance: with C1 running, input tokens per call drop measurably after a
prune on a long session; the digest still appears in the injection; no call
errors originate from the transform.

## C3 — Evaluate (feed removal)

Derived stats from verdicts + turn_metrics (SQL, no model):

- per path: accepted/corrected/rejected counts, last-verdict ts, recency
  weight; repeated corrections are negative.
- per group: acceptance rate (proposal quality).
- per prune: did corrections on pruned content follow? (prune effectiveness —
  a negative signal feeds the next retention decision).

These feed retention tiers (intent-network 08 section 3, "user control
overrides"): high-weight objects (frequent + accepted) are protected;
low-weight objects (single occurrence + corrected/rejected) become C2's
prune candidates. Promotion/demotion of mechanisms (05) is explicitly later —
C3 ships explainable stats only.

Acceptance: the stats view is non-empty after any verdicts; every prune
candidate list is explainable by row ("rejected, one occurrence").

## Honest limits

- Removal is structural first: verified slices, verdict history, recency.
  Deciding what is unnecessary by meaning stays judgment; we do not pretend
  otherwise.
- Token metrics depend on provider usage fields; absent fields are skipped.
- `messages.transform` rewriting is powerful and risky — bounded scope and
  fail-open are requirements, not options.

## Slices

| Slice | Content | Effort |
|---|---|---|
| **C1** | metrics tables + capture + views + policy manifest (tags only) | Short |
| **C2** | prune + compaction shaping, user-triggered, metric-proven | Medium |
| **C3** | verdict-derived stats + retention candidates feeding C2 | Medium |

Kill switch after each; each slice is independently useful.

## Open questions

1. Prune trigger: manual console only for C2, or also a token threshold?
2. Protected window: last N turns untouched (start with N = 6)?
3. Skill subject tags: keep for attribution, or defer entirely?

